/P 245 0 R
/E (The provided chart delineates the worsening "Twin Deficit" phenomenon in the U.S. from 2000 to 2025, illustrating a persistent structural imbalance where both the fiscal balance and the current account remain deeply in the negative as a percentage of GDP. While the current account deficit has remained relatively range-bound between -2% and -5%, the fiscal deficit exhibits extreme volatility, characterized by massive spikes during the 2008 financial crisis and a historic plunge to -15% during the 2020 pandemic. The divergence and continued widening of these deficits post-2020 suggest a regime of permanent fiscal expansion that necessitates continuous debt issuance, which for a macro researcher, signals a fundamental reliance on monetary debasement to sustain national solvency. This "twin" pressure exerts a long-term bearish force on the U.S. dollar's purchasing power, as the government is forced to fund domestic consumption and budget shortfalls through increased global liabilities and central bank intervention.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
