/E (The provided chart of the Federal Reserve's Total Assets \(WALCL\) indicates a potential structural shift in monetary policy, as the sustained Quantitative Tightening \(QT\) phase that began in 2022 appears to have found a definitive floor at approximately $6.5 trillion. After a multi-year drawdown from the $9 trillion peak, the recent uptick to $6.66 trillion in early 2026 serves as a critical macro signal that the "drainage" of liquidity from the financial system is reaching exhaustion. For a quantitative researcher, this inflection point suggests that the Fed is moving toward a "standing repo" or neutral balance sheet regime, effectively halting the contraction of the monetary base which has historically acted as a major headwind for risk assets. If this floor holds, it marks the return of a supportive liquidity environment, removing the primary barrier for a new expansionary cycle in decentralized and scarce assets like Bitcoin as the denominator once again begins to stabilize or expand.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
